window.CMA_QS=[{"id":"v2-su11-q1","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) A systematic approach to reaching targeted cost levels during\nvalue chain analysis is known as","options":["Value engineering.","Life-cycle costing.","Process value analysis.","Activity analysis.\n\nChain Analysis"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Value engineering is a means of reaching targeted cost levels.\nIt is a systematic approach to assessing all aspects of the\nvalue chain cost buildup for a product.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q2","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) A company develops computer programs to meet customers’\ncustomized needs. How should it classify payments to\nemployees who develop the programs?","options":["As direct costs and value-adding costs.","As direct costs, but not as value-adding costs.","As neither direct costs nor value-adding costs.","As value-adding costs, but not as direct costs."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Direct costs can be specifically associated with a single cost\nobject and assigned to it in an economically feasible way, i.e.,\ntraced to the object. Wages paid to employees that can be\nidentified with a specific finished project are direct costs.\nValue-adding costs are costs of activities that cannot be\neliminated without reducing the value of the product or service\nrequired by the customer. The amounts paid to the computer\nprogrammers add value to the computer programs.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q3","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) Which of the following is not a component of the value chain?","options":["Primary activities.","Secondary activities.\n\nChain Analysis","Support activities.","The product."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The value chain is a model for depicting the way in which\nevery function in a company adds value to the final product.\nPrimary activities deal with the product directly. Support\nactivities lend aid to the primary activity functions.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q4","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) A company has the following manufacturing-related activities:\n Setting up a machine for a new production run\n Assembling the final product\n Moving completed product to the warehouse\n Painting the assembled product\n Reworking defective parts\nWhich of these are value-added activities?","options":["Setting up a machine for new production run and assembling\nthe final product only.","Assembling the final product and painting the assembled\nproduct only.","Assembling the final product, painting the assembled product,\nand moving the completed product to the warehouse only.","Setting up a machine for a new production run, assembling the\nfinal product, painting the assembled product, and reworking\ndefective parts."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A value chain depicts how costs and customer value\n\nChain Analysis\naccumulate along a sequence of activities leading to an end\nproduct or service. Assembling the final product and painting\nthe assembled product are the only activities that add value\nfor the customer.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q5","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) The flow of materials and services from their original sources\nto final consumers is the","options":["Value chain.","Product chain.","Supply chain.","Value process.\n\nChain Analysis"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The supply chain usually encompasses more than one firm.\nFirms seeking to improve performance and reduce costs must\nanalyze all phases of the supply chain as well as the value\nchain.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q6","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) Which of the following is not a phase in a value-chain\nanalysis?","options":["Identify activities that are candidates for cost reduction.","Identify ways to generate additional customer value.","Identify means for improving product cost efficiency.","Identify the firm’s competitive advantage.\n\nChain Analysis"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The second step in a value-chain analysis is to determine how\neach value-creating activity can produce a competitive\nadvantage for the firm. This step has multiple substeps:\n1. Identify the firm’s competitive advantage (e.g., cost\nreduction, product differentiation) so that the firm’s\nposition in the industry’s value chain can be clarified.\n2. Identify the ways in which the firm’s value-creating\nactivities can generate additional customer value.\n3. Identify activities that are candidates for cost reduction\nor, in the case of non-core competencies, outsourcing.\n4. Identify value-adding ways in which the firm’s\nremaining activities can be linked.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q7","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) An outside consultant has been hired by a manufacturing firm\nto evaluate each of the firm’s major products, beginning with\nthe design of the products and continuing through the\nmanufacture, warehousing, distribution, sale, and service. The\nconsultant has also been requested to compare the\nmanufacturer’s major products with firms that are\nmanufacturing and marketing the same or similar products.\nThe consultant is to identify where customer value can be\nincreased; identify where costs can be reduced; and provide a\nbetter understanding of the linkages with customers, suppliers,\nand other firms in the industry. The type of analysis that the\nconsultant most likely has been asked to perform for the\nmanufacturing firm is called a","options":["Balanced scorecard study.","Benchmarking analysis.","SWOT (strengths, weakness, opportunities, threats) analysis.","Value-chain analysis."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Value-chain analysis for assessing competitive advantage is\nan integral part of the strategic planning process. Value-chain\nanalysis is a continuous process of gathering, evaluating, and\ncommunicating information for business decision making. A\n\nChain Analysis\nvalue chain depicts how customer value accumulates along a\nchain of activities that lead to an end product or service. A\nvalue chain consists of the activities required to research and\ndevelop, design, produce, market, deliver, and support its\nproduct. Extended value-chain analysis expands the view of\nthe parties involved to include those upstream (e.g., suppliers)\nand downstream (e.g., customers).","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q8","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) In the value creation chain model, the primary activities include","options":["Logistics, operations, marketing and sales, and service.","Procurement, infrastructure, operations, and service.","Procurement, infrastructure, operations, and technology\ndevelopment.","Procurement, infrastructure, human resources, and technology\ndevelopment."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The model consists of primary and supporting activities. The\nprimary activities are inbound logistics, operations, outbound\nlogistics, marketing and sales, and service. Inbound logistics\nactivities involve the firms’ capture of materials to be\nprocessed. Operations activities are conversion processes.\nOutbound logistics activities include shipment of products.\nMarketing and sales activities are the promotion and sale of\nfinal products. Service activities provide customer service. The\nfour support activities are infrastructure (e.g., administration,\nfinance, and planning), procurement, human resources, and\ntechnology development.\n\nChain Analysis","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q9","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) Customer relationship management is best defined as","options":["Coordination with members of the firm’s supply chain.","Maximizing short-term sales to customers.","Market sensing.","Maximizing customer loyalty by managing customer\n“touchpoints.”"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Customer relationship management can be defined as “the\nprocess of managing detailed information about individual\ncustomers and carefully managing all the customer\n‘touchpoints’ with the aim of maximizing customer loyalty.” Its\npurpose is to create optimal customer equity. Thus, the\nprocess involves more than merely attracting customers\n(through media advertising, direct mail, etc.) and satisfying\nthem (something competitors also may do).\n\nChain Analysis","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q10","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) Customer satisfaction is related to the degree of customer\nloyalty. High customer loyalty","options":["Requires a high degree of satisfaction of other stakeholders.","Results when quality, service, and price increase.","Depends on the effectiveness of the firm’s core business\nprocesses.","Is directly proportional to customer expectations."],"answer":"C","explanation":"High customer loyalty is an emotional as well as a rational\nbond that develops when a firm provides high customer value.\nTo obtain such loyalty, the firm needs to develop a value\nproposition that has superior competitiveness in the target\nmarket segment.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q11","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) The firm should emphasize customer retention","options":["By creating low switching costs.","By maximizing customer churn.","Although new customers are less costly than old customers.","Because the customer base is an intangible asset.\n\nChain Analysis"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Customer retention through customer satisfaction is a key to\nprofitability. The firm should emphasize customer retention\nbecause the customer base is an important intangible asset.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q12","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) Which one of the following best shows the sequence of\nbusiness functions in the value chain?","options":["Research and Development, Design of Products and\nProcesses, Production, Marketing, Distribution, Customer\nService.","Design of Products and Processes, Research and\nDevelopment, Finance, Production, Distribution, Customer\nService.","Design of Products and Processes, Finance, Research and\nDevelopment, Production, Distribution, Customer Service.\n\nChain Analysis","Research and Development, Finance, Design of Products and\nProcesses, Production, Distribution, Customer Service."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The correct sequence of business functions in the value chain\nis Research and Development, Design of Products and\nProcesses, Production, Marketing, Distribution, and Customer\nService.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q13","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) A company desires to prepare two sets of financial statements.\nConventional financial statements would be prepared along\nwith a set that is totally consistent with value-chain analysis.\nHow would customer service costs be treated in the two\nstatements?\nConventional Financial\nStatements\nValue-Chain Financial\nStatements","options":["Inventoriable cost Product cost","Inventoriable cost Non-product cost","Noninventoriable cost Product cost","Noninventoriable cost Non-product cost\n\nChain Analysis"],"answer":"C","explanation":"In conventional financial statements, customer service costs\nare generally part of sales and administrative costs and not\nassociated with a product. In value-chain statements,\ncustomer service is treated as part of the value chain and\ntherefore associated with product cost.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q14","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) Which one of the following lists of functions is in proper value-\nchain order?","options":["Research and development, marketing, and customer\nservices.","Production, marketing, and product design.","Production design, distribution, and marketing.","Research and development, customer service, and\ndistribution."],"answer":"A","explanation":"These functions are in the proper order while in the other\noptions the functions are out of order. Costs and customer\nvalue are added along the value-chain to provide an end\nproduct or service.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q15","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) A small computer manufacturer employs 25 plant workers in its\nmain manufacturing facility. The performance improvement\nteam has identified the following activities and relative time\ndemanded by each activity.\nActivities\nPercentage of\nworkers’\ntime","options":["Assembly of the computer components 25%\n\nChain Analysis","Installing software 40%","Inspecting and testing the assembled units 25%","Moving the finished units into the storage\narea 10%\nClassify the four activities as value-added and nonvalue-\nadded.\nValue-added Nonvalue-added\nA. A, B, and C D\nB. A and B C and D"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A value-added activity increases the value of a product or\nservice to the customer, while a nonvalue-added activity does\nnot. Activities A and B add value to the customer, while\nactivities C and D do not.\nC. A, B, and D C\nD. B and C A and D","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q16","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) Which one of the following is a value-added activity for a\ncompany that manufactures bicycles?","options":["Moving a tire to the rework area.\n\nChain Analysis","Placing a reworked brake on a bicycle.","Reworking a bicycle for the second time.","Inspecting a reworked bicycle."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A value-added activity contributes to customer satisfaction or\nmeets a need of the entity. A nonvalue-added activity does not\nmake such a contribution. It can be eliminated, reduced, or\nredesigned without impairing the quantity, quality, or\nresponsiveness of the product or service desired by\ncustomers or the entity. It is necessary to place a brake on a\nbicycle, whether the brake is reworked or new. The addition of\nthe brake is required before the bicycle can be sold, which\ncontributes to the customer’s satisfaction.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q17","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) A manufacturing company incurred the following costs during\nthe last quarter related to product development and\nproduction.\nMaterials storage $ 50,000\nProduct design 180,000\nMaterials handling and moving 41,000\nDirect materials 400,000\n\nChain Analysis\nQuality inspections 62,000\nWhat amount of costs should be classified as value-added?","options":["$491,000","$553,000","$580,000","$642,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Value-added activities increase the value of a product or\nservice to the customer. Examples include assembling the\nproduct and shipping it to the customer. Product design and\ndirect materials both qualify as value-added activities\n($180,000 + $400,000).","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q18","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) Which of the following is a primary activity on the value chain?","options":["Research and development (R&D).","Inventory management.","Information technology.","Human resources."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A value chain depicts how costs and customer value\naccumulate along a sequence of activities leading to an end\nproduct or service. The primary activities on the value chain\nare (1) R&D, (2) product design, (3) manufacturing, (4)\nmarketing and distribution, and (5) customer service.\n\nChain Analysis","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q19","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) A company’s value chain can best be described as","options":["An accumulation of costs and customer value.","A supply chain.","Value engineering.","Process analysis."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The value chain depicts how costs and customer value\naccumulate along a sequence of activities that lead to an end\nproduct or service. The primary activities on the value chain\nare (1) R&D, (2) product design, (3) manufacturing, (4)\nmarketing and distribution, and (5) customer service.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q20","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) A company’s new CEO has asked the CFO to work with the\nCOO to do a systematic assessment of all aspects of the\ncompany’s value chain for its major products. The purpose is\nto determine whether costs can be reduced without decreasing\ncustomer satisfaction. This type of analysis is\n\nChain Analysis","options":["An activity-based cost audit.","A continuous improvement process (CIP).","Value engineering.","Business process reengineering (BPR)."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Value engineering is the systematic assessment of all aspects\nof cost buildup along the value chain. The goal is to minimize\ncosts without negatively affecting customer satisfaction.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q21","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) The nonvalue-added activity for a manufacturing company is","options":["Assembling a product.","Adding new features to a product.","Shipping a product to a customer.","Inspection of a final product."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Nonvalue-added activities do not increase the value of the\nproduct or service even if they are necessary. For example,\ninspection, although important, does not increase the value of\nthe product.\n\nChain Analysis","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q22","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) A value chain is","options":["The allocation of overhead to products based on the activities\nthat drive the costs.","A method used to evaluate all aspects of performance,\nincluding financial and nonfinancial measures.","A group of activities connected with the production of a product\nor the performance of a service.","The approach used to decrease inventory costs by reducing\nthe amount of inventory on hand."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A value chain consists of all the value-added activities that\nlead to an end product or service. The primary activities on the\nvalue chain are (1) R&D, (2) product design, (3)\nmanufacturing, (4) marketing and distribution, and (5)\ncustomer service. Support activities include (1) human\nresources, (2) information technology, (3) contract\nmanagement, (4) inventory management, and (5) plant\nmaintenance.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q23","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) Which one of the following is a support activity on the value\nchain?","options":["Research and development (R&D).\n\nChain Analysis","Human resources.","Customer service.","Marketing and distribution."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The primary activities on the value chain are (1) R&D, (2)\nproduct design, (3) manufacturing, (4) marketing and\ndistribution, and (5) customer service. Support activities\ninclude (1) human resources, (2) information technology, (3)\ncontract management, (4) inventory management, and (5)\nplant maintenance.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q24","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) An accountant is working on classifying product costs as\nvalue-added and nonvalue-added. The chart below\nsummarizes the product costs.\nCost Type Cost Amount\nInventory storage $12,500\nManufacturing 44,675\nQuality control 12,350\nInspection of final product 7,260\nProduct assembly 16,450\nProduct shipment 8,575\n\nChain Analysis\nWhat is the total nonvalue-added activities cost?","options":["$12,500","$19,760","$24,850","$32,110"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Nonvalue-added activities do not increase the value of the\nproduct or service even if the activities are necessary.\nExamples include inspection of the final product and inventory\nstorage cost. The total nonvalue-added costs are $19,760\n($12,500 inventory storage cost + $7,260 inspection of final\nproduct cost).","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q25","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":25,"question":"25) Which of the following is a step in value-chain analysis?","options":["Identifying predetermined expectations of how much a unit of\ninput should cost.","Evaluating cost drivers.","Identifying the firm’s competitive advantage.","Evaluating progress toward objectives."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The steps in value-chain analysis are to (1) identify value-\ncreating activities and (2) determine how these activities can\n\nChain Analysis\ncreate competitive advantage. The second step involves (1)\nidentifying the firm’s competitive advantage, (2) determining\nhow the value-creating activities generate additional customer\nvalue, (3) identifying activities that reduce costs or can be\noutsourced, and (4) determining value-adding ways for the\nfirm’s remaining activities to be linked.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q26","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) Which one of the following is the best description of a supply\nchain?","options":["The materials suppliers.","The flow of materials on a production run from inventory to\nfinal product.","The flow of materials from their original sources to final\nconsumers.","The internal processes performed to create customer value.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\n11: (58) Business Process Improvement\n2: (32) Other Process Improvement Tools\nProcess Improvement Tools"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A supply chain is the movement of materials from the original\nsupplier source, through product production, to the retailers\nwho sell to the final customers. It generally consists of several\ncompanies.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q27","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) Which of the following is not a type of process?","options":["Make-to-stock.","Make-to-order.","Buffer.","Hybrid."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A buffer in the context of process analysis is a quantity of\nwork-in-process inventory that allows some stage(s) of the\noverall process to continue operating when an earlier stage\nbreaks down.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q28","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) The cost of scrap, rework, and tooling changes in a product\nquality cost system is categorized as a(n)","options":["Training cost.","External failure cost.","Internal failure cost.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","Prevention cost."],"answer":"C","explanation":"According to the IMA’s Management Accounting Glossary,\ninternal failure costs are incurred when detection of defective\nproducts occurs before shipment. Examples of internal failure\ncosts are scrap, rework, tooling changes, and downtime.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q29","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) Which of the following statements regarding benchmarking\nis false?","options":["Benchmarking involves continuously evaluating the practices\nof best-in-class organization and adapting company processes\nto incorporate the best of these practices.","Benchmarking, in practice, usually involves a company forming\nbenchmarking teams.","Benchmarking is an ongoing process that entails quantitative\nand qualitative measurement of the difference between the\ncompany’s performance of an activity and the performance by\nthe best in the world or the best in the industry.","The benchmarking organization against which a firm is\ncomparing itself must be a direct competitor.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Benchmarking is an ongoing process that involves quantitative\nand qualitative measurement of the difference between the\norganization’s performance of an activity and the performance\nby a best-in-class organization. The benchmarked\norganization need not be a direct competitor. The important\nconsideration is that it be an outstanding performer in its\nindustry.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q30","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) The four categories of costs associated with product quality\ncosts are","options":["External failure, internal failure, prevention, and carrying.","External failure, internal failure, prevention, and appraisal.","Warranty, product liability, training, and appraisal.","Warranty, product liability, prevention, and appraisal."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The four categories of quality costs are (1) prevention,\n(2) appraisal, (3) internal failure, and (4) external failure. Costs\nof prevention include attempts to avoid defective output, such\nas (1) employee training, (2) review of equipment design,\n(3) preventive maintenance, and (4) evaluation of suppliers.\nAppraisal includes quality control programs, inspection, and\ntesting. Internal failure costs are incurred when detection of\ndefective products occurs before shipment. They include costs\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nof (1) scrap, (2) rework, (3) tooling changes, and (4) downtime.\nExternal failure costs are incurred after the product has been\nshipped. They include the costs associated with warranties,\nproduct liability, and loss of customer goodwill.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q31","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) The cost of statistical quality control in a product quality cost\nsystem is categorized as a(n)","options":["Internal failure cost.","Training cost.","External failure cost.","Appraisal cost."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The following are the four categories of quality costs:\nprevention, appraisal, internal failure, and external failure (lost\nopportunity). Appraisal costs include quality control programs,\ninspection, and testing. However, some authorities regard\nstatistical quality and process control as preventive activities\nbecause they not only detect faulty work but also allow for\nadjustment of processes to avoid future defects.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q32","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) Listed below are selected line items from a cost of quality\nreport for last month.\nCategory Amount\nRework $ 725\nEquipment maintenance 1,154\nProduct testing 786\nProduct repair 695\nWhat is the total prevention and appraisal cost for last month?","options":["$786","$1,154","$1,940","$2,665"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The costs of prevention and appraisal are conformance costs\nthat serve as financial measures of internal performance.\nPrevention costs are incurred to prevent defective output.\nThese costs include (1) preventive maintenance, (2) employee\ntraining, (3) review of equipment design, and (4) evaluation of\nsuppliers. Appraisal costs are incurred to detect\nnonconforming output. They include such activities as\n(1) statistical quality control programs, (2) inspection, and\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\n(3) testing. The equipment maintenance cost of $1,154 is a\nprevention cost. The product testing cost of $786 is an\nappraisal cost. Their sum is $1,940.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q33","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) Which of the following quality costs are nonconformance\ncosts?","options":["Systems development costs.","Costs of inspecting in-process items.","Environmental costs.","Costs of quality circles."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Nonconformance costs include internal and external failure\ncosts. External failure costs include environmental costs, e.g.,\nfines for violations of environmental laws and loss of customer\ngoodwill.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q34","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) An example of an internal nonfinancial benchmark is the","options":["Labor rate of comparably skilled employees at a major\ncompetitor’s plant.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","Average actual cost per pound of a specific product at the\ncompany’s most efficient plant becoming the benchmark for\nthe company’s other plants.","Company setting a benchmark of $50,000 for employee\ntraining programs at each of the company’s plants.","Percentage of customer orders delivered on time at the\ncompany’s most efficient plant becoming the benchmark for\nthe company’s other plants."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Benchmarking involves continuously evaluating the principles\nof best-in-class organizations and adapting company\nprocesses to incorporate the best of these practices. It\nanalyzes and measures the key outputs of a business process\nor function against the best and also identifies the underlying\nkey actions and root causes that contribute to the performance\ndifference. The percentage of orders delivered on time at the\ncompany’s most efficient plant is an example of an internal\nnonfinancial benchmark.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q35","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) A retailer, which operates eight discount store chains, is\nseeking to reduce the costs of its purchasing activities through\nreengineering and heavier use of electronic data interchange\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\n(EDI). Which of the following benchmarks techniques would be\nappropriate in this situation?\nI. A comparison of the purchasing costs and practices of\neach of the store chains to identify their internal “best\nin class.”\nII. A comparison of the practices of the retailer to those of\nanother retailer whose practices are often considered\n“best in class.”\nIII. A comparison of the best practices of the retailer to\nthose of an international airline whose practices are\noften considered “best in class.”\nIV. An in-depth review of a retail trade association\npublication on successful EDI applications.","options":["II and IV only.","I and II only.","I and IV only.","I, II, III, and IV."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The retailer would benefit from all of these benchmark\ntechniques as all would identify best practices. The IMA’s\nStatement on Management Accounting (SMA) Effective\nBenchmarking states, “Benchmarking involves continuously\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nevaluating the practices of best-in-class organizations and\nadapting company processes to incorporate the best of these\npractices.” It “analyzes and measures the key outputs of a\nbusiness process or function against the best and also\nidentifies the underlying key actions and root causes that\ncontribute to the performance difference.”","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q36","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) A firm manufactures dishwashers and incurs the following\nselected costs.\nMonthly equipment maintenance $5,000\nLabor to ensure the closed dishwasher doors form a\ntight seal 8,000\nUnplanned manufacturing machinery repairs 9,000\nLabor to reattach doors due to misalignment found\nduring inspection 1,500\nLabor to reattach doors due to misalignment found by\ncustomers 1,200\nWhen preparing a cost of quality report, the amount spent in\nthe internal failure category would total","options":["$1,500","$10,500\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","$11,700","$23,500"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The only two internal failure costs incurred are the unplanned\nmanufacturing machinery repairs and the labor to reattach\ndoors due to misalignment found by inspection, which result in\n$10,500 for internal failure ($9,000 + $1,500).","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q37","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) A quality cost report reveals the following information.\nPrevention costs 10%\nAppraisal costs 15%\nInternal failure costs 20%\nExternal failure costs 55%\nTotal quality costs 100%\nWhich one of the following should be emphasized in order\nto most effectively improve overall cost of quality\nperformance?","options":["Quality training.","Inspections.","Rework.","Customer support.\nFact Pattern: Listed below are costs of quality that a manufacturing\ncompany has incurred throughout its operations. The company plans\nto prepare a report that classifies these costs into the following four\ncategories: preventive costs, appraisal costs, internal failure costs,\nand external failure costs.\nCost Items Amou\nnt\nDesign reviews $275,0\n00\nFinished goods returned due to\nfailure\n55,000\nFreight on replacement finished\ngoods\n27,000\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nLabor inspection during\nmanufacturing\n75,000\nLabor inspection of raw materials 32,000\nManufacturing product-testing\nlabor\n63,000\nManufacturing rework labor and\noverhead\n150,00\n0\nMaterials used in warranty\nrepairs\n68,000\nProcess engineering 180,00\n0\nProduct-liability claims 145,00\n0\nProduct-testing equipment 35,000\nRepairs to equipment due to brea\nkdowns 22,000\nScheduled equipment\nmaintenance\n90,000\nScrap material 125,00\n0\nTraining of manufacturing\nworkers\n156,00\n0\n\nProcess Improvement Tools"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The largest portion of total cost of quality is external failure.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nQuality training, which will result in higher quality awareness\non the part of all employees, is the appropriate corrective\naction. Quality training is a prevention cost, and an increase in\nprevention should reduce all other cost categories.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q38","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) The dollar amount of the costs of quality classified as\npreventive costs for the manufacturing firm would be","options":["$643,000","$701,000","$736,000","$768,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Prevention attempts to avoid defective output, e.g., by\nemployee training, review of equipment design, preventive\nmaintenance, and evaluation of suppliers. Accordingly, the\npreventive costs equal $701,000 ($275,000 design reviews +\n$180,000 process engineering + $90,000 scheduled\nmaintenance + $156,000 training).","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q39","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) The costs of quality that are incurred in detecting units of\nproduct that do not conform to product specifications are\nreferred to as","options":["Preventive costs.","Appraisal costs.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","Internal failure costs.","External failure costs."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The categories of quality costs include conformance costs\n(prevention and appraisal) and nonconformance costs\n(internal failure and external failure). Appraisal costs embrace\nsuch activities as statistical quality control programs,\ninspection, and testing. Thus, the cost of detecting\nnonconforming products is an appraisal cost.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q40","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) All of the following are examples of benchmarking\nstandards except","options":["The performance of the unit during the previous year.","The best performance of the unit in comparable past periods.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","A comparison with a similar unit within the same company.","The best performance of a competitor with a similar operation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Benchmarking involves continuously evaluating the practices\nof best-in-class organizations and adapting company\nprocesses to incorporate the best of these practices. Thus,\nbenchmarking involves setting standards high. Arbitrarily using\nthe unit’s prior period performance runs counter to the goal of\nbenchmarking.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q41","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) A company implemented a benchmarking program to compare\nitself to others in the industry. Through this program, the\ncompany management team discovered that a larger\ncompetitor has a lower overhead per unit sold. Based on this\ninformation, management concluded that steps must be taken\nto reduce overhead to remain competitive. Which one of the\nfollowing is the best critique of this conclusion?","options":["Benchmarking should be performed with companies of similar\nsize and sales.","Fixed overhead is difficult to control and should not be\nbenchmarked.","Cost per unit is just one area of competitiveness; others should\nbe looked at.","Companies operate very differently, and comparisons should\nnot be made.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The ideal benchmarking partner should be similar in size and\nstructure. A larger competitor may be benefiting from\neconomies of scale, which a smaller company cannot match.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q42","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) A manufacturer of high-technology consumer goods incurred\nthe following quality-related expenses last year.\nEquipment maintenance $ 5,000\nSpoilage 10,000\nLiability claims 50,000\nSupplier evaluations 5,000\nScrap 20,000\nCustomer support 50,000\nFinished product testing 25,000\nWhat is the total cost related to prevention?","options":["$165,000","$35,000","$10,000","$5,000\n\nProcess Improvement Tools"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Equipment maintenance and supplier evaluations are both\nprevention costs, as both are proactive measures to ensure\nthe ability of the production facility to continue to operate.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q43","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) A manufacturer that wants to improve its staging process\ncompares its procedures against the check-in process for a\nmajor airline. Which of the following tools is the manufacturer\nusing?","options":["Total quality management.","Statistical process control.","Economic value added.","Benchmarking."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Benchmarking is a primary tool used in quality management. It\nis a means of helping organizations with productivity\nmanagement and business process analysis. Benchmarking\ninvolves analysis and measurement of key outputs against\nthose of the best organizations. This procedure also involves\nidentifying the underlying key actions and causes that\ncontribute to the performance difference. The benchmark\nneed not be a competitor or even a similar entity. Process\n(function) benchmarking studies operations of organizations\nwith similar processes regardless of industry. Thus, a\ncomparison to procedures against the check-in process for a\nmajor airline is an example of benchmarking.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q44","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) Which one of the following would be classified as an internal\nfailure cost on a quality cost report?","options":["Depreciation of inspection equipment.","Returns and allowances.","Net cost of scrap.","Final product testing and inspection."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Internal failure costs occur when defective products are\ndetected before shipment. Examples are scrap, rework,\ntooling charges, downtime, redesign of products or processes,\nlost output, and lost learning opportunities. Thus, the net cost\nof scrap would be classified as an internal failure cost on the\nquality report.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q45","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) Which of the following statements are true with respect to\ncontinuous improvement?\nI. Improvements should be made continuously until the\ngoal is reached.\nII. The continuous improvement should involve\nmanagement and workers.\nIII. Standards should be evaluated regularly, and\nimprovements should be ongoing.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nIV. Continuous improvement initiatives should involve\nprimarily the workers.","options":["I and II only.","I and IV only.","II and III only.","III and IV only."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Improvements should be ongoing, with involvement by\nmanagement and workers.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q46","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) Which one of the following statements about benchmarking\nis not correct?","options":["A budget may provide a benchmark that motivates employees\nto meet a company’s goals.","A company may appropriately use benchmarks to evaluate\nemployee or unit performance.","Benchmarking may include comparing a unit’s achievements\nagainst other units within the company.","Comparing a unit’s results against industry benchmarks is not\nusually considered as useful as comparing results to the\nbudget.\n\nProcess Improvement Tools"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Comparing a unit’s results against industry benchmarks is\nusually considered as useful as comparing results to the\nbudget. The budget helps in comparing actual results to the\nexpectations of the company. Industry benchmarks help in\ncomparing actual results to industry performance. Comparison\nto industry is just as important as company expectations in\nevaluating a unit’s results.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q47","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) A company is currently performing a cost of quality analysis of\none of its facilities. The following are costs compiled by the\nfacility accountant:\nInspection $1,500\nWarranty repair 2,800\nTesting of new materials 400\nProduct testing 950\nSpoilage 645\nScrap 150\nPreventive equipment maintenance 590\nLiability claims 1,870\nRework 1,285\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nTotal internal failure cost is","options":["$2,080","$2,785","$4,945","$5,955"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Internal failure costs occur when defective products are\ndetected before shipment. The internal failure costs include\nthe cost of abnormal spoilage, scrap, and rework. Thus, the\ntotal internal failure cost is $2,080 ($645 + $150 + $1,285).","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q48","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) For purposes of cost control, process reengineering\nis most similar to","options":["Activity-based costing.","Kaizen budgeting.","Variance analysis.","Value-chain analysis."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Process reengineering and value chain analysis are similar\ndue to their focus on inherent features in an organization’s\nbusiness processes. Both rework an organization’s design to\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\neither improve customer service, reduce costs, or become\nmore competitive.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q49","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) The process improvement tool that compares company\npractices with those of best-in-class organizations is","options":["Value chain analysis.","Enterprise performance management.","The balanced scorecard.","Benchmarking."],"answer":"D","explanation":"According to the IMA, “benchmarking involves continuously\nevaluating the practices of best-in-class organizations and\nadapting company processes to incorporate the best of these\npractices.” It “analyzes and measures the key outputs of a\nbusiness process or function against the best and also\nidentifies underlying key actions and root causes that\ncontribute to the performance difference.” Benchmarking is an\nongoing process that entails quantitative and qualitative\nmeasurement of the difference between the company’s\nperformance of an activity and the performance by the best in\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nthe world. The benchmark organization need not be a\ncompetitor.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q50","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) A manufacturer experiences significant downtime on its\nassembly line. This delay is an example of what type of quality\ncost?","options":["Prevention.","Internal failure.","Appraisal.","External failure."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Nonconformance costs include costs of internal failure and\nexternal failure. Internal failure costs occur when defective\nproducts are detected before shipment. Examples include\nscrap, rework, tooling changes, downtime, and correcting\nproblems.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q51","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":25,"question":"25) An accountant prepared the following cost of quality report\nsummary for the most recent month of operations for his\ncompany.\nCategory Amount\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nMaterials waste $1,750\nProduct testing 1,235\nProduct returns 2,050\nMachine breakdowns 3,416\nWhat is the total internal failure cost for the month?","options":["$1,235","$1,750","$3,285","$5,166"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Internal failure costs of quality occur when defective products\nare detected before shipment. Examples include scrap,\nrework, tooling changes, downtime, and correcting problems.\nMaterials waste and machine breakdowns result in internal\nfailure costs. The total is $5,166 ($1,750 materials waste +\n$3,416 machine breakdowns).","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q52","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) The CEO of a company has initiated a project that will involve\ncompletely rethinking how business functions are performed to\ndeliver value to customers. It will likely involve a radical\nrestructuring of the business. This type of analysis is\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","options":["An activity-based cost audit.","Continuous improvement.","Value engineering.","Business process reengineering (BPR)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"BPR involves process innovation and core process redesign.\nThus, it requires a complete rethinking of how business\nfunctions are performed.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q53","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":27,"question":"27) A controller prepared the following cost of quality report\nsummary for the most recent month of operations for her\ncompany.\nCategory Amount\nEmployee quality training $ 2,750\nEquipment downtime 1,500\nWarranty obligations 9,050\nEnvironmental law violation fine 10,000\nWhat is the total external failure cost for the month?","options":["$9,050","$10,000","$10,550\n\nProcess Improvement Tools","$19,050"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Nonconformance costs include internal and external failure\ncosts. Examples of external failure costs are those incurred for\ncustomer service complaints, product return, warranty\nobligations, and environmental law violations. The total for the\nmonth is $19,050 ($9,050 warranty obligations + $10,000\nenvironmental law violation fine).","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q54","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":28,"question":"28) Which one of the following statements correctly describes\nprocess analysis?","options":["A method of radically innovating how business functions are\nperformed and how a company is organized.","A continuous effort to incrementally improve processes to\nmake them more effective and more efficient.","A method of analyzing the operational steps taken to complete\nwork with the goal of identifying what is working well and what\nneeds improvement.","A systematic assessment of all aspects of the value chain with\nthe goal of minimizing costs without negatively affecting\ncustomer satisfaction."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Process analysis is a means of linking an entity’s internal\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nprocesses with its overall strategies. It is a method of\nreviewing the procedures in place to achieve business goals.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q55","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":29,"question":"29) The CEO of a company formed a team to perform a process\nanalysis. Team members include employees from the\naccounting department and the operations department. In their\nanalysis, they found that the slowest process was the entire\nproduction process. As part of their report to the CEO, they\nplan to recommend adding additional capacity for this process.\nWhat is the correct term for the process slowing the production\nprocess?","options":["Loose.","Tight.","Batch.","Bottleneck."],"answer":"D","explanation":"A bottleneck is the process that slows the entire production\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nprocess. It often has limited capacity. After additional capacity\nis added, the bottleneck shifts to the next slowest process.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q56","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":30,"question":"30) Which one of the following statements correctly describes\nbenchmarking?","options":["Identifying activities that add customer value and help create\ncompetitive advantage.","Reimagining how business functions are performed with the\ngoal of radically reorganizing the organization.","Evaluating processes with an organization to determine which\nones are slowing down production.","Identifying strategies, tactics, methods, and processes of the\nmost successful companies in an industry."],"answer":"D","explanation":"“Benchmarking involves continuously evaluating the practices\nof best-in-class organizations and adapting company\nprocesses to incorporate the best of these practices.” It\n“analyzes and measures the key outputs of a business\nprocess or function against the best and also identifies\nunderlying key actions and root causes that contribute to the\nperformance difference.” Benchmarking is an ongoing process\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\nthat entails quantitative and qualitative measurement of the\ndifference between the company’s performance of an activity\nand the performance by the best in the world. The benchmark\norganization need not be a competitor.","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"v2-su11-q57","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":31,"question":"31) The new CEO of a multinational consumer goods company\nformed a benchmarking team with the goal of improving the\neffectiveness of its key business processes. The company is\nmarginally profitable, and the CEO believes it could be much\nmore efficient and effective in meeting customer needs. The\ncompany is based in the U.S. and has operations in Europe,\nthe Middle East, Africa, and Asia. The Asia market is its\nbiggest market. Which is most likely the best benchmark\norganization?","options":["The leader in the telecommunications industry.","A competitor of the same size and same level of success.","The leader in the consumer goods industry.","The Asia division operations."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Benchmarking involves evaluating the practices of best-in-\nclass organizations and adapting company processes to\ninclude the best of those practices. The benchmark\n\nProcess Improvement Tools\norganization need not be a competitor. It may be a different\nindustry or segment of the same company. The best\ncomparison is often with the leader in the same industry,\npreferably one of a comparable size.","sectionId":"11.2"},{"id":"v2-su11-q58","volume":2,"unit":11,"unitTitle":"Business Process Improvement","subunit":null,"questionNumber":32,"question":"32) The controller is reviewing a summary cost of quality report\nwith the chief operating officer. The controller states that the\nnonconformance costs greatly exceed the conformance costs.\nWhich costs are nonconformance costs?","options":["Prevention costs and internal failure costs.","Internal failure costs and external failure costs.","Appraisal costs and external failure costs.","Prevention costs and appraisal costs.\n\nCorrelation and Regression\n12: (155) Analysis, Forecasting, and Strategy\n1: (28) Correlation and Regression\n2: (34) Learning Curve Analysis\n3: (51) Expected Value and Sensitivity Analysis\n4: (28) Strategic Management\n5: (14) Strategic Planning\n12: (155) Analysis, Forecasting, and Strategy\n1: (28) Correlation and Regression\nSubunit 1: Correlation and Regression"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Nonconformance costs are costs of internal failure costs (a\nfinancial measure of internal performance) and external failure\ncosts (a financial measure of customer satisfaction).\nConformance costs are prevention and appraisal costs\n(financial measures of internal performance).","sectionId":"11.1"},{"id":"aq.tqm.004_1809","question":"Which of the following correctly orders the kaizen activity cycle?","options":["Plan, Do, Check, Act","Plan, Do, Act, Check","Do, Check, Act, Plan","Plan, Act, Do, Check"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. The kaizen activity cycle often follows a Plan, Do, Check, Act approach (PDCA). (1) The Plan phase of the PDCA cycle is about assessing a current or proposed process and establishing a plan to improve it. (2) In the Do phase, small changes are put in place and data are gathered in order to determine if the changes made are effective and results are as expected. (3) The Check phase is where the evaluation on the research data takes place, decisions regarding adjustments are made, and additional doing and data gathering takes place until it becomes clear what improvements will best provide the desired results. (4) Finally, the Act phase is effectively the go/no go decision in the kaizen cycle. Assuming the organization determines the process can and should be improved, then the new process is rolled out, new performance standards are set, and the organization “acts” on the change.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.tqm.003_1809","question":"Which of the following correctly explains the concept of continuous improvement (kaizen) and how it relates to implementing standards and quality improvements?","options":["Because kaizen is focused on taking small steps, this means that kaizen management doesn't have large goals.","The key kaizen concept in using ideal standards is focused on measuring the variance from the standard as the performance measure.","The expectation in kaizen is continual small improvements that target ideal standards that will eventually yield large results in terms of overall improvement in productivity.","The typical practice of total quality management using kaizen is based on practical standards of performance."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. Total quality management is often described using a single Japanese word, kaizen, which means “improvement” or “good improvement.” In the context of total quality management (TQM), the kaizen concept is more typically thought of as “continuous improvement that takes place in a constant series of small steps.” This continual process of improvement by small steps is in contrast to more radical business process improvement concepts such as business process reengineering (BPR), which is an approach that seeks for large, dramatic, and often painful transformations in the organization. The expectation in kaizen is continual small improvements that target ideal standards that will eventually yield large results in terms of overall improvement in productivity.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.tqm.008_1809","question":"Which of the following describes the quality of relevance in the accounting process?","options":["The quality of relevance concept is measured in terms of reducing errors in reports.","The concept that quality of relevance is measured in terms of how fast the accounting team is able to close out the books at the end of each period and provide a report on operations.","The concept that the information generated by the accounting process should impact the decision making of someone involved in the management process of planning, controlling, and evaluating operations.","The concept that any accounting information that does not add value to managers and decision makers should be eliminated."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. For most organizations the most important quality of accounting is relevance. Accounting must have high relevance in the management process of planning, controlling, and evaluating operations. When it doesn't have relevance, when accounting is not a key input to decisions involving operations and strategy, then accountants need to carefully consider how to make the accounting process more efficient. Accounting is a core activity. When it's done well, the organization is greatly strengthened.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.tqm.005_1809","question":"All of the following are components of the costs of quality except:","options":["External failure costs","Transportation costs","Prevention costs","Internal failure costs"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. Transportation costs are not a component of the costs of quality.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.tqm.002_1809","question":"Which of the following correctly explains the relationship among continuous improvement techniques, activity-based management (ABM), and quality performance?","options":["The ABM framework accomplishes the management objectives of the organization.","If total quality management is the organization's objective, then management can use ABM to identify activities that represent a quality-focused organization, and track costs and performance measures on those activities.","The ABM model is itself a specific management concept.","There is no relationship between ABM and continuous improvement techniques or quality performance."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. It is important to understand that the ABM model is not itself a management concept. ABM is an integrated framework that management can use to establish and maintain a reporting structure to support the management objectives of the organization. For example, if total quality management is the organization's objective, then management will identify activities that represent a quality-focused organization, and track costs and performance measures on those activities (as well as use those activities to assign costs to cost objects).","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.tqm.006_1809","question":"Most organizations spend significant costs on inspection and testing, as well as sampling of raw materials, work in process, and finished goods and services. What type of quality costs are these expenditures?","options":["Appraisal costs","Prevention costs","External failure costs","Internal failure costs"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. Appraisal costs represent what the organization spends on inspection, testing, and sampling of raw materials, work in process, and finished goods and services. These costs include overhead expenses for quality inspectors, costs to adjust measuring and test equipment, and costs of associated supplies and materials.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.tqm.009_1809","question":"All of the following are ways to apply the “lean” concept to the accounting process except:","options":["eliminating managers’ direct access to accounting data.","eliminating reports that have little management use.","eliminating the time a report waits for another accounting operation before it's ready for managers.","eliminating the effort and process required for managers to request or receive accounting reports."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. The “lean” concept emphasizes the absolute elimination of waste in the process, in other words, to eliminate anything that does not add value for the customer. The customers of accounting are managers and decision makers who use the accounting information. Applying the lean concept to accounting includes eliminating reports that have little management use, eliminating the time a report waits for another accounting operation before it's ready for managers, and eliminating the effort and process required for managers to request or receive accounting reports (typically by facilitating managers with direct access to accounting data). Eliminating waste in accounting can significantly reduce the cost of accounting processes.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.tqm.001_1809","question":"All of the following are true statements about activity-based management (ABM) except:","options":["ABM is an expansion of the ABC (activity-based costing) model.","ABM emphasizes the view that organizations should be oriented around value-added activities and should focus the management process on these activities.","ABM identifies cost drivers that affect resource consumption and performance measures related to quality and timeliness.","The ABM model has a horizontal relationship with activities, meaning that activities function as a vehicle to accurately carry costs spent on resources in the organization and assign those costs to the organization's cost objects."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. This is a description of activity-based costing (ABC). The ABM model is represented as having a vertical, not horizontal, relationship with activities. ABM goes beyond using activities as a cost assignment vehicle by establishing activities as the focus of management. ABM is focused on reporting cost drivers and performance measures to help manage each activity. The ABC model is depicted as a horizontal relationship.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.theor.con.007_1809","question":"All of the following statements regarding throughput accounting are true except:","options":["Operational expense represents all costs other than raw materials used to convert inventory into throughput.","Inventory represents all the money tied up in the production system, including purchased inventory, machines, buildings, and other assets.","Throughput accounting supports a very specific and extremely short-term managerial view of an operation.","Throughput is computed as revenue minus extremely variable costs, which typically are the cost of raw materials and direct labor."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. Throughput, often called throughput margin, is computed as revenue minus extremely variable costs, which typically are the cost of raw materials and perhaps other extremely variable costs such as selling commissions. All other costs, including direct and indirect labor, are considered fixed and are not included in the computation of throughput margin. This definition is based on a very short-term view of costs, perhaps as short as one-to-four weeks.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.theor.con.001_1809","question":"The theory of constraints (TOC) has all of the following perspectives of inventory except:","options":["Inventory slows down the production process, leading to delayed or even lost sales.","Unneeded inventory is a costly investment that doesn't generate an income return.","Inventory is not really an asset in the production process, but is often a barrier to achieving the organization's main goals.","Inventory highlights problems with quality."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. Inventory hides, not highlights, problems with quality. If there are quality problems with raw materials purchased or if the production process creates quality problems in work-in-process inventory, those quality problems are hidden within the inventory “pile” until discovered when the inventory eventually moves into the next process. Until discovered, the quality problem continues in the organization, leading to needless rework and scrap. Even worse, quality problems in the hands of customers reduce future revenue.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.theor.con.002_1809","question":"What is the core difference between theory of constraints (TOC) and just-in-time (JIT) management systems?","options":["TOC focuses the process on the bottleneck and uses a buffer of inventory to keep the bottleneck at full capacity while JIT seeks to remove all inventory.","TOC focuses on a horizontal process while JIT focuses on a vertical process.","TOC recognizes a problem with inventory while JIT does not.","TOC focuses on optimizing each operation as a coordinated system to produce only what is needed while JIT focuses on optimizing each operation by maximizing its individual production capacity."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. The core difference between TOC and JIT is that TOC focuses the process on the bottleneck and uses a buffer of inventory to keep the bottleneck at full capacity. Optimizing the system using TOC is a function of optimizing the bottleneck. Put another way, any production lost at the bottleneck operation represents a permanent loss in the entire organization. In contrast, all other operations have, by definition, more capacity than the bottleneck operation. Hence, not only should non-bottleneck operations be controlled to produce only at the capacity level of the bottleneck, non-bottleneck operations should use their excess capacity to do everything possible to facilitate the bottleneck's ability to always operate at its full capacity.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.theor.con.003_1809","question":"The theory of constraints (TOC) model is described as a drum-buffer-rope system. What does the rope represent in the TOC model?","options":["The rope represents the one place in the organization where work-in-process inventory is valuable, which is directly in front of the bottleneck operation.","The rope is a scheduling and signaling process for downstream operations.","The rope represents restraints placed on upstream operations not to overwhelm the bottleneck operation with so much inventory that cost, quality, and timeliness problems start becoming an issue.","The rope is a method of increasing the capacity of the bottleneck operation."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. The rope represents restraints placed on upstream operations to not overwhelm the bottleneck operation with so much inventory that cost, quality, and timeliness problems start becoming an issue. Upstream operations need to deliver just enough in-process inventory to the bottleneck buffer to ensure that the bottleneck is never idle based on problems upstream in the organization.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.theor.con.005_1809","question":"Which of the following correctly describes Step 2 (Determine how to exploit the constraint) of the theory of constraints (TOC) five-step process?","options":["Determining whether the constraint is an internal process constraint, internal policy constraint, external material constraint, or external market constraint","Maximizing the money-making capacity of the bottleneck","Shifting focus to the new constraint once the bottleneck shifts","Exploring the means to increase the capacity of the constraint"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. Step 2 (Determine how to exploit the constraint) describes that the organization must maximize the moneymaking capacity of the bottleneck by prioritizing the products that best use the constraint to build throughput margin. This is done once the bottleneck is identified.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.theor.con.006_1809","question":"Which of the five steps for the theory of constraints (TOC) is based on making all other operations and their capacities secondary to the needs of the bottleneck?","options":["Step 1: Identify the constraint.","Step 4: Elevate the constraint.","Step 3: Subordinate all other operations to the constraint.","Step 2: Determine how to exploit the constraint."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. Step 3 is when all other operations and their capacities become secondary to the needs of the bottleneck. This takes place once the organization determines how the constraint is to be exploited to maximize throughput for the organization.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.theor.con.004_1809","question":"Which of the following correctly orders the theory of constraints (TOC) five-step process?","options":["Identify the constraint; Determine how to exploit the constraint; Subordinate all other operations to the constraint; Elevate the constraint; When the constraint is broken, go back to Step 1.","Elevate the constraint; Determine how to exploit the constraint; Subordinate all other operations to the constraint; Identify the constraint; When the constraint is broken, go back to Step 1.","Identify the constraint; Elevate the constraint; Determine how to exploit the constraint; Subordinate all other operations to the constraint; When the constraint is broken, go back to Step 1.","Identify the constraint; Subordinate all other operations to the constraint; Determine how to exploit the constraint; Elevate the constraint; When the constraint is broken, go back to Step 1."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. The TOC five steps are Step 1: Identify the constraint; Step 2: Determine how to exploit the constraint; Step 3: Subordinate all other operations to the constraint; Step 4: Elevate the constraint; Step 5: When the constraint is broken, go back to Step 1.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.003_1809","question":"All of the following are support activities in value chain analysis except:","options":["service.","technology.","HR Management.","firm infrastructure."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. Service is a primary activity, not a support activity.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.008_1809","question":"What are the three primary areas for improvement when using best practice analysis and benchmarking for processes and products?","options":["Costs, quality, timeliness","Inbound logistics, outbound logistics, HR management","Operations, marketing and sales, and service","Procurement, technology, and firm infrastructure"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. Organizations can benchmark both processes and products for improvement. Generally, there are three possible areas for improvement: costs, quality, and timeliness. The need to improve any or all of these three aspects of processes and products can inspire a benchmarking study.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.005_1809","question":"From the perspective of the customer, which of the following is a value-added activity?","options":["The company building a relationship with a new supplier","The company installing a new ERP system","The company sending service techs to install the customer's purchase","The company providing cutting-edge training to the accounting department"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. The company sending service techs to install the customer's purchase is a value-added activity from the perspective of the customer. This answer falls under service, which is a primary activity.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.006_1809","question":"All of the following describe business process reengineering (BPR) except:","options":["BPR is performed in order to better support the organization's strategy and to reduce costs.","BPR's focus is to obliterate unproductive management layers, wipe out redundancies, and radically remodel processes.","BPR focuses on fine-tuning operations and adjusting teams and processes.","BPR represents a total review and reconstruction of the organization's core processes."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. Business process reengineering (BPR) represents a total review and reconstruction of the organization's core processes in order to better support the organization's strategy and to reduce costs. This approach goes way beyond simply fine-tuning operations and adjusting teams and processes. The focus in BPR is to obliterate unproductive management layers, wipe out redundancies, and radically remodel processes.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.007_1809","question":"Which of the following correctly orders the steps in the business process reengineering cycle?","options":["Identify process, test and implement process, design new process, review and analyze process.","Identify process, design new process, review and analyze process, test and implement process.","Review and analyze process, identify process, design new process, test and implement process.","Identify process, review and analyze process, design new process, test and implement process."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. The business process reengineering cycle goes as follows: (1) A current process is identified for total review and restructuring. (2) The process is carefully reviewed and analyzed for alignment with the organization's strategy and for efficiency. (3) The process is then updated, redesigned, or entirely replaced. (4) Testing and implementation of the updated or new process provides feedback to the BPR cycle and helps identify the next process to be reengineered.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.001_1809","question":"Which of the following most accurately defines value chain analysis?","options":["An approach used by an organization to allocate capacity costs while tracking and reporting the fixed costs of idle capacity in the organization's production process","An approach used by an organization to evaluate its internal activities and identify how each is involved in creating and capturing value for the organization's customers","A system based on the idea that inventory is not really an asset in the production process, but is often a barrier to achieving the organization's main goals","A system that supports a very specific and extremely short-term managerial view of an operation—the incremental value from a more effective employment of a constrained resource"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. Value chain analysis is an approach used by an organization to evaluate its internal activities and identify how each is involved in creating and capturing value for the organization's customers.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.004_1809","question":"All of the following describe how value chain analysis helps a firm better understand its strategy and competitive advantage except:","options":["Value chain analysis is an approach used by an organization to evaluate its internal activities and identify how each is involved in creating and capturing value for the organization's customers.","Value chain analysis is an approach used by an organization to compare their practices to the best practices of competitors and noncompetitors both inside and outside the industry.","Value chain analysis involves a careful review of all primary and support activities to determine how each activity relates to the organization's strategy and how it contributes to the organization's profit margin.","Value chain analysis pushes organizations to (1) identify what is valued in the marketplace in terms of what customers or potential customers are willing to pay to receive and (2) decide what basic approach (low-cost product, differentiated product, niche market product) they are going to take to create value that customers are willing to pay to receive."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. Benchmarking, not value chain analysis, is an approach used by an organization to compare their practices to the best practices of competitors and noncompetitors both inside and outside the industry.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.010_1809","question":"All of the following are benefits of strategic benchmarking except:","options":["identifying and building core competencies.","establishing better risk management systems.","developing new business lines.","performing less research on companies outside the industry."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. Benchmarking against the best strategic practices of competitors and noncompetitors both inside and outside of the industry can be a valuable aspect of process analysis and improvement. The benchmarking discipline does not involve performing less research on companies outside the industry.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.009_1809","question":"Which of the following correctly orders the steps in the benchmarking process?","options":["Select project and organize the benchmarking team; identify and research the benchmarking target; document the current strategy, process, or product; analyze benchmarking data and identify improvements; implement the recommendations and recalibrate as needed.","Document the current strategy, process, or product; select project and organize the benchmarking team; identify and research the benchmarking target; analyze benchmarking data and identify improvements; implement the recommendations and recalibrate as needed.","Select project and organize the benchmarking team; document the current strategy, process, or product; identify and research the benchmarking target; analyze benchmarking data and identify improvements; implement the recommendations and recalibrate as needed.","Identify and research the benchmarking target; document the current strategy, process, or product; select project and organize the benchmarking team; analyze benchmarking data and identify improvements; implement the recommendations and recalibrate as needed."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. The steps involved in a benchmarking process are: (1) select project and organize the benchmarking team; (2) document the current strategy, process, or product; (3) identify and research the benchmarking target; (4) analyze benchmarking data and identify improvements; (5) implement the recommendations and recalibrate as needed.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.val.ca.002_1809","question":"All of the following are primary activities in value chain analysis except:","options":["procurement.","operations.","outbound logistics.","marketing and sales."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. Procurement is a support activity, not a primary activity.","unit":11,"subunit":"11.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0}];